/// Shipped knowledge catalog and operational corpus. Content bytes and source references determine chunk identities.
@template
automation knowledgeSeedCatalog {
  return {
  computerUse: [
    {
      SourceRef: "computerUse:fleetAppSetup",
      Text: "Fleet app setup is a work goal for a developer-supported app: Claude Code (claude-code) or Codex (codex). Its input names the selected registrationId and clusterUrl. Read workerStatus, match that exact registration, and use its requireLabels for every operation and approval. Never substitute another machine or a workbench. Inspect installation and the official app authentication status with bounded host commands. Install a missing app only through its official installer after inspecting the platform and required permissions. Never read or echo token files, copy credentials, or use another account. Use the app's official sign-in flow and hand browser/account authentication to the person when required; preserve the work while waiting. Cockpit's allow-list is separate from authentication: request explicit approval before the cluster-scoped memql worker apps --allow command. Never enable broad computer control or edit policy files to bypass a refusal. Re-check the app's official status and Cockpit's report. A launched installer or sign-in page is not success: report installed, signed-in and allowed separately, including any blocker. Stop after two failed attempts at the same step and ask for the missing information."
    },
    {
      SourceRef: "computerUse:overview",
      Text: "Computer Use lets an agent work on the person's enrolled computer through MemQL Cockpit. Prefer native reads and APIs or the run's isolated workbench when personal files or that machine are unnecessary. The available tools are workerHost for headless commands, files and HTTP; workerComputer for screen, mouse and keyboard; workerStatus for availability; and requestComputerUseScope for a durable approval. Availability is not permission. Explain the purpose, selected machine and exact access in ordinary language; keep wire-level tool names out of user-facing instructions."
    },
    {
      SourceRef: "computerUse:toolSurfaces",
      Text: "Computer Use execution surfaces have different contracts. workerHost supports exec, fs_read, fs_list, fs_stat, fs_write and http_fetch. Prefer a bounded headless operation when it reliably completes the authorized task. workerComputer is for actions that genuinely require the person's desktop; inspect the screen before acting. workerStatus reports eligible machines across cluster replicas: headlessOnline permits host-tool routing, while computerUseOnline requires desktop control with a display. Neither result authorizes an action. Check the live tool contract and use the required machine labels; do not assume a connected machine supports a particular operation."
    },
    {
      SourceRef: "computerUse:machineSelection",
      Text: "Selecting one or several fleet computers. Start with workerStatus: its machines list reports names, headlessOnline, computerUseOnline, computerPermissionSupported, platform, homeDirectory when reported, and exact requireLabels selectors. Use a machine the person explicitly named or already chose for this work. The browser's host is not established by the phrase 'this machine', an inference server or the first connected worker. If the target is unclear, use requestUserFeedback with actual machine names; use kind=multi when the work may involve several machines. Preserve choices in the work context and reuse them on resume. Ask again only for a new target or unresolved ambiguity. Pass each selected machine's exact requireLabels to both requestComputerUseScope and execution. Several machines require separate targeted calls and approvals. A machine choice does not grant access. If the selected machine is offline or requires a Cockpit update, report that fact instead of substituting another computer."
    },
    {
      SourceRef: "computerUse:headlessFirst",
      Text: "Choosing a computer operation. Prefer direct capabilities, CLI commands or headless filesystem operations when they can complete the task; use workerComputer when those routes cannot do it. For directory listings use workerHost fs_list with args.path and an observe directory-read permission. For home listings copy the selected machine's homeDirectory into BOTH operation.args.path and operation.directory, with operation.tool=workerHost and operation.action=fs_list. Use the same path and requireLabels for workerHost after approval. This is the worker account's home; if the person means a different OS account, clarify its path. If homeDirectory is absent, ask for the exact path or a Cockpit update; never guess /home, /home/user, a username, ~ or $HOME. For exec, args.cmd is the exact command and args.cwd is its absolute working directory. Request the matching operation before dispatch and reuse confirmed applicable approvals. The workbench is a separate cluster workspace and cannot inspect the person's home directory. If headless and desktop routes cannot proceed, ask a focused question naming the remaining blocker. Do not repeatedly search the catalog for tools already supplied, or mistake model inference on a machine for permission to read files or execute commands."
    },
    {
      SourceRef: "computerUse:capabilityChoice",
      Text: "Choosing a permitted capability. Identify the current section's required action, destination and observable result before selecting a tool. Use discoverCapabilities when available and inspect the exposed contract; do not invent an operation or assume an executable exists. Choose a supported native operation, command, API or browser interaction that can reach the intended destination within current authority. The run's workbench and the person's enrolled computer are separate locations. A connected machine, available executable or signed-in account does not itself authorize an operation. For a known file, prefer the permitted native read and reuse a successful archived result when current content is not required. Follow computerUse:fileReads and computerUse:archivedFileReads for byte limits and coverage; a returned prefix is not the whole file. Verify the requested effect from its result or a bounded follow-up read, using the run journal to distinguish completed actions from plans and uncertain attempts."
    },
    {
      SourceRef: "computerUse:unavailablePrerequisite",
      Text: "Diagnosing an unavailable prerequisite. Preserve the actual refusal and distinguish a missing executable, a local machine policy denial, missing service authentication, and missing operation permission. A Nexus approval records permission for an operation; it does not itself install software, establish service authentication or change local policy. A missing command alone is not evidence that the task needs another machine. When the operation is authorized, an independently permitted API, native capability or browser can be an alternative; changing tools must not bypass the same denied access or effect. Do not wrap a denied executable in another shell or interpreter to evade its policy. Follow existing task, repository and operator authorization through supported dependency installation or configuration actions; ask only when the required authority is missing. A local policy denial remains binding until resolved through the supported, authorized operator path. Never print credentials to diagnose authentication. If no permitted route exists, name the precise prerequisite and preserve completed work. Retry after the cause changes; after an uncertain side effect, inspect current state before repeating it."
    },
    {
      SourceRef: "computerUse:fileReads",
      Text: "Reading known files on the enrolled computer. Use workerHost with action fs_read and args {path, maxBytes?}; path must be the explicit absolute path inside the approved directory. Typed directory reads accept maxBytes from 1 through 5242880 bytes and default to 5242880 (5 MiB). The reader returns content and size, where size is bytes returned, not the full file size. When reported by the worker, fileSize is the opened file's byte size before the read. For a default prefix read, truncated says whether the returned prefix is shorter than that size; an exact-size read reports false. Use these fields to judge coverage. Older workers may omit them; use fs_stat with {path} to check the full file size when that metadata is unavailable. These are byte counts, not character counts, and do not guarantee a snapshot if the file changes concurrently. Workers advertising workerHost.fs_read contract 2 also accept offsetBytes with maxBytes, startLine with lineCount, or search with a nonempty literal and optional maxMatches. These modes cannot be combined. Search and line ranges accept maxScanBytes (up to 16 MiB, default 5 MiB); search returns matching whole lines with line numbers and byte offsets, bounded by maxBytes and maxMatches. complete is true when a prefix or offset read reaches EOF, when a line read covers the requested lines or reaches EOF, or when a search reaches EOF without withholding matches. It does not imply coverage before an offset or before the requested lines. truncated or changedDuringRead means coverage is incomplete. An empty incomplete search cannot prove absence. nextOffsetBytes locates the byte continuation; sha256 identifies returned bytes, not the entire file. The engine requires the advertised contract before routing an extended read or asking for its permission. Workers without it support only a prefix read using path and maxBytes. A limited prefix is not the whole file. fs_list with {path} lists one directory, not a recursive tree. A matching saved directory permission covers these reads subject to the selected machine, local consent and path policy. workbenchHost reads the separate cluster workspace using workspace-relative paths; it does not read the enrolled computer's files."
    },
    {
      SourceRef: "computerUse:archivedFileReads",
      Text: "Finding a passage in an already read file. When a successful workerHost fs_read result is archived, reuse that result through recallWorkHistory rather than requesting the same file again or a shell command just to filter it. Use the archived result's checkpoint and messageIndex with a search phrase, omitting offset to locate the passage; continue with nextOffset only when more of that passage is needed. These offsets page the saved result, not the live file, and cannot recover bytes the original limited read never returned. If the file changed after the read and current contents matter, read it again under the same applicable permission. For a known unread file, prefer the permitted fs_read before considering shell inspection. There is no native recursive file-content search on workerHost: a shell search, even a read-only one, is exec and requires its exact command approval. Never treat a directory-read permission as permission to run a command or broaden it to avoid review."
    },
    {
      SourceRef: "computerUse:scopeTiers",
      Text: "Computer Use permissions should describe the smallest reviewable operation. For workerHost fs_read, fs_list or fs_stat, request a typed directory permission with an explicit absolute path and directory. It covers only these read operations within the approved directory, checked on the selected machine against real paths and symlinks. For exec, request the exact command, absolute cwd and timeoutSec (1 through 600 seconds). Exact-command approval supports only cmd, cwd and optional timeoutSec: environment overrides, stdin and extra options are refused. Never remove operation or substitute a broad scope to get around a refusal. The required requestedScope is observe for directory reads and full for exec; these labels do not expand the typed permission. Other computer actions retain their existing run-scoped approval contract and cannot use a saved directory or command permission."
    },
    {
      SourceRef: "computerUse:perTaskApproval",
      Text: "Requesting Computer Use permission. For directory reads or exec, operation is mandatory: call requestComputerUseScope with intent, requestedScope, summary and operation={tool: workerHost, action, args, directory?}. Supply the exact operation args, including the absolute path or cwd; directory selects the read boundary. Explain what will run and why. Supply the selected machine's exact requireLabels from workerStatus. The server refuses an ambiguous machine choice and presents the immutable scope in Nexus -> Approvals. Once authorizes one dispatch in this run; Always is offered only for a supported typed scope and remains bound to the owner, agent, selected registration and machine, and exact command or directory. A matching saved permission can be reused only when the server confirms it. Omitting operation is an argument error: correct the call rather than asking the person again. Only desktop interaction, fs_write and http_fetch use explicit sessionKind=desktop, file_write or http with expiring run access; never select a session kind for a directory read or command. When status is awaiting_user, preserve progress and wait for the recorded decision; do not execute early or repeatedly create approvals. An approved result permits only the matching operation, not unrelated work."
    },
    {
      SourceRef: "computerUse:postApprovalExecution",
      Text: "Executing approved Computer Use work. Resume from the durable run and its recorded receipts, not from an obsolete planApprovedTrigger or a guessed chat confirmation. Reuse the approved scope only for a matching call; retain the reviewed command, cwd and timeout rather than changing inputs after approval. The platform verifies the current owner, agent, run and selected machine at dispatch. A typed permission never grants a broad fallback. Cockpit's local consent window, deny policy and workspace boundary remain independent ceilings; Nexus approval cannot override them. If a changed operation needs different permission, request that exact scope. Verify the returned result before reporting progress, and use the run journal to avoid repeating an already completed side effect."
    },
    {
      SourceRef: "computerUse:planOutcomeSemantics",
      Text: "Computer Use outcomes are evidence for the current work run. A successful invocation proves only the action it performed; it does not prove the whole goal is complete. Check the requested artifacts, command output and acceptance criteria, and report remaining work honestly. A refused, failed, cancelled or timed-out call is not completion. Preserve receipts and diagnostics through pauses and retries. Do not manufacture canvas completion cards or claim that a Plan succeeded because one worker call returned success. Use the current Ask response and durable run state to explain what finished, what failed and what still needs a decision."
    },
    {
      SourceRef: "computerUse:availabilityStates",
      Text: "Computer Use readiness has separate gates. workerStatus reports eligible connected workers, not local consent or an approved operation. If no eligible machine is online, explain which connection or capability is missing. If consent_required is returned, ask the operator to open the named cluster's bounded local consent window in Cockpit; a saved Nexus permission cannot open it. If the machine refuses a path or operation, surface the stated local policy limitation. Do not invent scope settings, restart flags or an environment repair to bypass it. Saved permissions can be reviewed and revoked in Nexus -> Approvals -> Saved permissions. Revocation, enrollment changes and unsupported action contracts must fail closed."
    },
    {
      SourceRef: "computerUse:errorBudget",
      Text: "Computer Use refusals and discussion. Preserve the exact actionable refusal for consent_required, computer_permission_refused, computer_permission_path_refused or computer_permission_unavailable. These require a person or an available supported machine, not an environment-repair approval. If a permission request fails before a durable approval exists, explain that no request was accepted and retry only when its actual cause is addressed. A rejected or expired approval grants nothing. Discuss in Ask returns to the original conversation with the selected approval; Send guidance gives a correction to that same parked run and can trigger bounded replanning. An ordinary answer, discussion or guidance is never a permission grant. Do not silently broaden scope or repeatedly ask for the same refused access."
    }
  ],
